"The prognosis is influenced by several documented factors. The surgical findings are favorable, showing a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2), no extraprostatic extension, negative surgical margins, and no seminal vesicle invasion. The absence of lymphovascular invasion is also noted. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 5.7 and the lack of lymph node removal, as nodal status is documented as 'no lymph nodes were removed,' which widens uncertainty. The biopsy finding, showing an Acinar pattern, is noted, and there is no documented difference between the biopsy grade and the prostatectomy specimen. Missing postoperative PSA or follow-up data significantly limits the certainty of the trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its subsequent trend would be crucial in revising this prognosis. The current heuristic horizon suggests a range for recurrence. The fixed estimated horizon is 47.33 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 30.76–63.89 months; its coverage has not been validated."
